# Architecture Summary (for AI Agents)

## Service List

| Service | Purpose |
|---------|---------|
| Skill Orchestrator | Workflow control: parses arguments, routes to mode, spawns agents, manages re-spawn |
| Agent Executor | Domain execution: generates SDLC artifacts (SRS, HLD, LLD, IMP, TST) |
| Hook Engine | Event automation: responds to Claude Code lifecycle events |
| Eval Engine | Empirical testing: benchmarks skills against baselines with assertions |
| Sprint Manager | Planning tools: manages roadmap, backlog, board artifacts |
| Doc Generator | Output production: writes architecture docs, feature specs, tech-design |

## Communication Rules

- Skill Orchestrator → Agent Executor: via `Agent()` tool call with structured brief
- Hook Engine → Claude Code: via shell script exit codes + stdout
- Eval Engine → Skills: via file-based assertions (read eval config, compare output)
- Sprint Manager → Doc Generator: via shared file state (board.md, backlog.md)

## Key Constraints

1. Never specify output paths in agent briefs — agents know their templates
2. Gate-verifier reads only; never modifies files
3. Maximum 3 re-spawns per phase on gate rejection
4. Re-spawn the preceding phase agent, not the gate
5. All scout reports referenced as shared input
6. No sandbox — agents operate directly on project filesystem
